e me an overall  summary  of how it seemed to her how it seems  hurt friends and various points which  stuck in her mind which we hadn't  discussed and things which perhaps i  hadn't known about  one of the most interesting  ones of these was the following i  after i got out in the countryside and  left beijing was traveling in the  countryside i began to be more and more  aware of the element of xenophobia of  the effort that the government was  making to turn  the blame for everything on  foreigners and particularly the united  states  and this was a  exasperated by  the fact that we had given refuge in the  embassy to fangliji the famous chinese  dissident and for reasons i don't quite  understand that very name and that man  is anathema to deng xiaoping and the  chinese really were vibrating about the  idea of him being in  in the embassy and they were demanding  that he be released demanding all these  different things  and it occurred to me i knew that we  would not release a man that we'd given  political embassy uh asylum to  that the one thing the chinese might try  was to seize a hostage  and looking around the country uh  all the americans had left the country  by that time  uh very few uh people left in the  embassy  some  regular working newspaper men in  in beijing but i stuck out like sort of  like a sore thumb because i was very  prominent in china and i thought the  chinese would think well he's a very  prominent person why don't you grab him  and then we can work a trade i didn't  really want to be involved in anything  of that kind and i didn't say anything  to charlotte about this because i didn't  want her worrying about that  but lo and behold  she got a call one day from somebody who  wanted to know if she had heard anything  in the state department that i was in  some personal danger  she was very upset by that  and then that very evening  dan rather mentioned on the cbs  broadcast that the american  the state department is worried about  the seizure of a hostage  who might be traded off for family xi  so i realized then that this what i  thought was a vagrant thought in my mind  was one  which  which really uh was in other people's  minds and  one of the first things i did when i got  back to  taconic was to sit down with charlotte  and explain to her how this all had come  about and i didn't really know and i  don't know this day how the state  department got worried about that  question but i think it was simply using  the same kind of reason and logic that i  was using in analyzing the situation  what day did you leave beijing  i left beijing uh on the fifth of uh the  fifth of june what day was the attack  and the attack was uh the third and  fourth and where did you go when you  left well uh the first thing we had to  do was to get out of beijing which was  not at all easy because uh the beijing  hotel where we were was was surrounded  and barricaded we were really prisoners  in the hotel but we got a  a chinese driver who was very familiar  with the the little alleyways the  hutongs that make up the central part of  beijing we managed to pick our way  through those back alleys until we  managed to get a clear highway the  reason for the difficulty was there were  so many barricades across all the  streets and so much military activity  and shooting that we didn't want to  and the driver didn't want to expose his  car to it and we didn't want to expose  us to it we got out to the airport which  is deserted because nobody could get out  of the city and we flew down to wuhan  about a two hour flight out of  out of beijing and there we begin a trip  through the countryside which enabled me  to see how the people outside of beijing  were responding to the events  and whether indeed they knew what had  happened uh in beijing they had their  regular commercial service still  underway during this time oh yes yes  indeed  and um  there had been many rumors that the  the air service had been closed down  but it hasn't it  for the first time in all the years i've  been going to china i rode on a chinese  airplane that was not filled  the chinese airlines had a i think  a practice of not flying until they sold  every seat in the plane but they  couldn't sell them all now and what was  your reason for going out into the  country after  you'd been in beijing during this period  and did you take all your japanese  camera crew with you yes we went out to  fulfill the uh the countryside segment  of our uh our  our planned itinerary we went out to to  film in various cities that were  important to the history of the people's  republic  to interview people in various these  various locations  the first place we went to was wuhan  where the famous bridge across the  yangtze had been built  and  we found that wuhan had been the scene  of some very big  manifestations in fact they were still  going on when we arrived there there had  been  there had been  big demonstrations in wuhan on the very  afternoon and sunday afternoon after the  after the massacre already word had  reached  wuhan and the students had come out and  carried out demonstrations and large  crowds gathered  we went into the heart of town and found  that we could hardly get through the  shopping  center because there were four big  meetings being held by students and  there were big posters on the walls uh  telling about what had happened in  chiang min and listing the names of  wuhan students who had been killed in  the in the massacre  we drove out in the town and discovered  we bumped into a big funeral parade  coming back that had held a a ceremony  for the victims of chennai men and they  had these great big chinese  paper wreaths that they used for their  funerals  and there must have been several  thousand people in in that  and so we knew immediately that  that the people in wuhan at any rate  which is a great city of several million  people  knew all about  what had happened in the square and as  we went along deeper and deeper into the  countryside we found the same thing was  true  all of this had been gotten out the word  about what had happened there had been  gotten out by the students ahead of the  government propaganda this is before the  government began to claim that nobody  was killed there was no massacre intent  on men and so we then found  that people had already gotten the truth  in their systems and when the government  lies began to come out  they were taken as lies by the people  they didn't stand up and shout about it  but they knew perfectly well the  government was trying to put something  over on them and they weren't buying it  how did you stay in touch with the  outside world during that time well i  stayed in touch in two ways one by  shortwave radio i listened to the bbc  and the voa constantly and also my  telephone calls to my colleagues in  beijing  and my japanese  colleagues has called their  correspondence in beijing or in tokyo  and so we were quite well informed as to  what was going on and what kind of a job  do you think the bbc and the voa do  i think they did a magnificent job i was  i was utterly astounded  from the very beginning um at uh  let's say 4 a.m or 3 30 a.m on on  june 4th  i was in my hotel room and  changing avenue was dark i could see  movement there i could hear the shooting  from  tiananmen i couldn't tell at that moment  whether the troops had taken over the  square whether a battle was going on or  what and i flicked on  the voa and i got the last part of a of  a broadcast in which they were saying  the troops had come into into the square  and they had been firing as they came in  and a lot of people had been killed so  then i i could interpret what was  happening in the square  as the as the day wore on again and  again  when i was not clear as to what was  actually happening on the streets after  all i had limited vision  only down  a few blocks to the east and a few  blocks to the west  i would turn on t