ink when you have something  like this on your mind  and  when you're when you're really revved up  about something you it jet lag tends to  vanish i i can't recall a trip in which  i had less problems with jet lag do you  want to go back to china yes very much  so i um  i'm working on a much bigger and broader  project than chiang mai what i've been  working on for the last three years is a  book  on the dung shopping period in china  essentially  what has happened in china since mao  died  when did he die he died in 76 in  september  exactly  13 years ago uh this month and um he  left china in a dreadful state of chaos  dung came in and he put things together  and he got china going in a remarkable  way and he had 10 straight years in  which he did  he changed china in many ways  as it hadn't been changed in thousands  of years he brought prosperity to the  countryside by eliminating the communes  and enabling the farmers to go into  private farming and private business and  and make money  very good for china  and then this comes along  an unfortunate and contradictory  end really to his career  something which damages enormously his  achievements and it will take them a  long time to pick up on it all of that  goes in this book which i'm now writing  about a third of that book was written  before  jenna mindari  and i have i think all the essential  research for the other segments  which all been blocked out  last three years i spent an awful lot of  time in china working on this  but i should get back  and to take the temperature again and  make some political soundings so that  i'm a little bit more certain  of  the  direction in which china is is going to  emerge after dung  passes from the scene and hopefully get  a little clearer idea who his successor  may be  i'm not sure this is accurate tell me if  i'm misreading it you got a sense that  going into this whole experience of you  being over there that you thought the  students were going to win you come o