e me an overall  summary  of how it seemed to her how it seems  hurt friends and various points which  stuck in her mind which we hadn't  discussed and things which perhaps i  hadn't known about  one of the most interesting  ones of these was the following i  after i got out in the countryside and  left beijing was traveling in the  countryside i began to be more and more  aware of the element of xenophobia of  the effort that the government was  making to turn  the blame for everything on  foreigners and particularly the united  states  and this was a  exasperated by  the fact that we had given refuge in the  embassy to fangliji the famous chinese  dissident and for reasons i don't quite  understand that very name and that man  is anathema to deng xiaoping and the  chinese really were vibrating about the  idea of him being in  in the embassy and they were demanding  that he be released demanding all these  different things  and it occurred to me i knew that we  would not release a man that we'd given  political embassy uh asylum to  that the one thing the chinese might try  was to seize a hostage  and looking around the country uh  all the americans had left the country  by that time  uh very few uh people left in the  embassy  some  regular working newspaper men in  in beijing but i stuck out like sort of  like a sore thumb because i was very  prominent in china and i thought the  chinese would think well he's a very  prominent person why don't you grab him  and then we can work a trade i didn't  really want to be involved in anything  of that kind and i didn't say anything  to charlotte about this because i didn't  want her worrying about that  but lo and behold  she got a call one day from somebody who  wanted to know if she had heard anything  in the state department that i was in  some personal danger  she was very upset by that  and then that very evening  dan rather mentioned on the cbs  broadcast that the american  the state department is worried about  the seizure of a hostage  who might be traded off for family xi  so i realized then that this what i  thought was a vagrant thought in my mind  was one  which  which really uh was in other people's  minds and  one of the first things i did when i got  back to  taconic was to sit down with charlotte  and explain to her how this all had come  about and i didn't really know and i  don't know this day how the state  department got worried about that  question but i think it was simply using  the same kind of reason and logic that i  was using in analyzing the situation  what day did you leave beijing  i left beijing uh on the fifth of uh the  fifth of june what day was the attack  and the attack was uh the third and  fourth and where did you go when you  left well uh the first thing we had to  do was to get out of beijing which was  not at all easy because uh the beijing  hotel where we were was was surrounded  and barricaded we were really prisoners  in the hotel but we got a  a chinese driver who was very familiar  with the the little alleyways the  hutongs that make up the central part of  beijing we managed to pick our way  through those back alleys until we  managed to get a clear highway the  reason for the difficulty was there were  so many barricades across all the  streets and so much military activity  and shooting that we didn't want to  and the driver didn't want to expose his  car to it and we didn't want to expose  us to it we got out to the airport which  is deserted because nobody could get out  of the city and we flew down to wuhan  about a two hour flight out of  out of beijing and there we begin a trip  through the countryside which enabled me  to see how the people outside of beijing  were responding to the events  and whether indeed they knew what had  happened uh in beijing they had their  regular commercial service still  underway during this time oh yes yes  indeed  and um  there had been many rumors that the  the air service had been closed down  but it hasn't it  for the first time in all the years i've  been going to china i rode on a chinese  airplane that was not filled  the chinese airlines had a i think  a practice of not flying until they sold  every seat in the plane but they  couldn't sell them all now and what was  your reason for going out into the  country after  you'd been in beijing during this period  and did you take all your japanese  camera crew with you yes we went out to  fulfill the uh the countryside segment  of our uh our  our planned itinerary we went out to to  film in various cities that were  important to the history of the people's  republic  to interview people in various these  various locations  the first place we went to was wuhan  where the famous bridge across the  yangtze had been built  and  we found that wuhan had been the scene  of some very big  manifestations in fact they were still  going on when we arrived there there had  been  there had been  big demonstrations in wuhan on the very  afternoon and sunday afternoon after the  after the massacre already word had  reached  wuhan and the students had come out and  carried out demonstrations and large  crowds gathered  we went into the heart of town and found  that we could hardly get through the  shopping  center because there were four big  meetings being held by students and  there were big posters on the walls uh  telling about what had happened in  chiang min and listing the names of  wuhan students who had been killed in  the in the massacre  we drove out in the town and discovered  we bumped into a big funeral parade  coming back that had held a a ceremony  for the victims of chennai men and they  had these great big chinese  paper wreaths that they used for their  funerals  and there must have been several  thousand people in in that  and so we knew immediately that  that the people in wuhan at any rate  which is a great city of several million  people  knew all about  what had happened in the square and as  we went along deeper and deeper into the  countryside we found the same thing was  true  all of this had been gotten out the word  about what had happened there had been  gotten out by the students ahead of the  government propaganda this is before the  government began to claim that nobody  was killed there was no massacre intent  on men and so we then found  that people had already gotten the truth  in their systems and when the government  lies began to come out  they were taken as lies by the people  they didn't stand up and shout about it  but they knew perfectly well the  government was trying to put something  over on them and they weren't buying it  how did you stay in touch with the  outside world during that time well i  stayed in touch in two ways one by  shortwave radio i listened to the bbc  and the voa constantly and also my  telephone calls to my colleagues in  beijing  and my japanese  colleagues has called their  correspondence in beijing or in tokyo  and so we were quite well informed as to  what was going on and what kind of a job  do you think the bbc and the voa do  i think they did a magnificent job i was  i was utterly astounded  from the very beginning um at uh  let's say 4 a.m or 3 30 a.m on on  june 4th  i was in my hotel room and  changing avenue was dark i could see  movement there i could hear the shooting  from  tiananmen i couldn't tell at that moment  whether the troops had taken over the  square whether a battle was going on or  what and i flicked on  the voa and i got the last part of a of  a broadcast in which they were saying  the troops had come into into the square  and they had been firing as they came in  and a lot of people had been killed so  then i i could interpret what was  happening in the square  as the as the day wore on again and  again  when i was not clear as to what was  actually happening on the streets after  all i had limited vision  only down  a few blocks to the east and a few  blocks to the west  i would turn on the  radio or tv i didn't turn the tv the  radio to either a bbc or voa and see  what they were reporting and if on it  matched up exactly with what i was  hearing and seeing  i think they did a great job i don't  know anybody outside of the chinese  government who contends that their  reports were exaggerated in any way  sometimes they weren't entirely accurate  but you don't always have a hundred  percent accuracy in the middle of a  battle uh too much is going on  and in the early stages for example  uh they under-reported the casualties uh  that was that was we all did that i  myself estimated i couldn't believe that  more than 100 150 people at most have  been killed and i was astounded when i  began to hear reports of several  thousand but this was because i hadn't  realized how many people had been killed  by the armored columns as they  plunged into the city with their guns  going into solid masses of people and  literally blasted their way through  those crowds  after i had a couple of first-hand  descriptions  of what happened when the tanks and the  armored troop carriers came in then i  could readily see  why the toll would be as high as that it  uh it needn't have been but if you're  going to go in and you're shooting  you've got an awful lot of targets to  shoot at by the way when did you have  your first discussion that this was a  book i mean you said that you didn't  start out thinking about it oh well i  tell you i that came  the day after i got back to new york i  i uh called my editor  roger donald ed little brown and i said  i've got something to talk to you about  and he said i've got something to talk  to you about well it turned out we had  the same idea  tiananmen diary he didn't know i'd kept  a diary but he thought that a book on  this subject a quick book  would be useful and i thought i said  i've got it half written he said  wonderful we're going to make that an  instant book so we've never tried that  before but we're going to do it this  time because the interest is here we  want the book to come out while people  are eager to  know exactly step by step what happened  so i said fine  and i proceeded then  to complete the book which i had about  half copied i completed the book and  wrote the  the final chapter explaining as best i  could well i thought it happened and all  that  called up roger and he came down from  boston stopped off at taconic  and spent the day  editing something which i've never  handed an editor in my life this was the  raw copy i hadn't had it i hadn't had it  copied over i hadn't copied it over  myself it was full of interlining and  all the rest of it he said that doesn't  make any difference i'm a good editor i  can i can read all through all that and  he did and at the end of the day he said  fine it's a book  and i had answered two or three  questions and put little inserts in  he took it away  turned it into their copy reading  department  and  i believe the day after the fourth of  july was turned over to the printer and  we had bound books by  the fourth or fifth of august  and actual publication date was  september 1st yes publication was  september 1st  and  is it too late have things changed since  you finished this or do you find this  still relevant i think every word of it  is very relevant there are some things  which i've found out since which are not  in the book and i wish they were  more details of  how the troops  blasted their way in a little bit on the  political background a few little facts  here and there but there is nothing  wrong in the book the i think it has the  right  mood it has the right explanation for  why  the chinese acted as they did and i  think i have accurately portrayed  the response of the students and most  particularly of the people of beijing  and at no time during this trip did you  find yourself out of energy  by myself out of energy  you were able to deal with all this jet  lag and traveling and all this kind of  stuff i think when you have something  like this on your mind  and  when you're when you're really revved up  about something you it jet lag tends to  vanish i i can't recall a trip in which  i had less problems with jet lag do you  want to go back to china yes very much  so i um  i'm working on a much bigger and broader  project than chiang mai what i've been  working on for the last three years is a  book  on the dung shopping period in china  essentially  what has happened in china since mao  died  when did he die he died in 76 in  september  exactly  13 years ago uh this month and um he  left china in a dreadful state of chaos  dung came in and he put things together  and he got china going in a remarkable  way and he had 10 straight years in  which he did  he changed china in many ways  as it hadn't been changed in thousands  of years he brought prosperity to the  countryside by eliminating the communes  and enabling the farmers to go into  private farming and private business and  and make money  very good for china  and then this comes along  an unfortunate and contradictory  end really to his career  something which damages enormously his  achievements and it will take them a  long time to pick up on it all of that  goes in this book which i'm now writing  about a third of that book was written  before  jenna mindari  and i have i think all the essential  research for the other segments  which all been blocked out  last three years i spent an awful lot of  time in china working on this  but i should get back  and to take the temperature again and  make some political soundings so that  i'm a little bit more certain  of  the  direction in which china is is going to  emerge after dung  passes from the scene and hopefully get  a little clearer idea who his successor  may be  i'm not sure this is accurate tell me if  i'm misreading it you got a sense that  going into this whole experience of you  being over there that you thought the  students were going to win you come out  more pessimistic saying it may be a long  time did you change your view during  this yes i did very much so i thought  that i thought  from all i had learned before going over  there and indeed what i learned after i  got out it got over there in the short  period of time  that i had it looked to me as though the  students were winning or at least their  ideas were winning they didn't  necessarily have to have that dialogue  with the government they might not have  it but the government  which had the same general objectives  was going to be spurred and pushed along  in that direction by the student  movement  and this i must say  was the view of my chinese friends they  all believed that  extraordinarily and beyond their  expectations this was what was going to  happen and they were almost deliriously  happy about it  well as we know  i was wrong and they were wrong and it  all came to a bitter bloody end  and it's not going to be that way  the next round when it comes and i'm  sure it's going to come  with dong's death is going to be a  struggle of a different type it's going  to be led i don't think by students but  by adults probably by the people of  beijing i don't know who their leaders  will be they didn't have any leaders in  this  in in the tiananmen thing they were just  all of them out there but they're be  leaders  and the army will not be one unit as it  was supporting dong but probably  conflicting  uh generals and divisions  it may there may be some civil war i  don't know there may be some  splintering up of china at least  temporarily uh  reversion  i hope it doesn't happen but some of the  chinese think maybe a reversion to  warlordism or something of that kind  out of it all i'm sure there will come a  new china which will take up the beat  and continue to march towards something  better but it isn't going to happen  tomorrow it isn't going to happen two  years from now i can remember during the  june uh  the those days that you write about  a television reporter standing in  tiananmen square saying china will never  be the same again  this is before the military moved in you  think that the reporters were too  optimistic  and  do you think it ever will be the same  again  well  they're absolutely right that china will  never be the same again but that isn't  what they were thinking about they were  thinking that china was going to be the  new china that would follow would be in  the model  of what they saw in the student movement  and i remember very well dan rather  saying that i remember betty about lord  the wife of the ex-ambassador saying  words the same effect and i know many  people many china scholars in this  country  and  particularly a lot of chinese  students and and younger chinese saying  that  they were they were wrong about it how  can so many people be so wrong  well let me give you another thing  the cultural revolution which lasted  about 10 years and  embraced probably  well undoubtedly the worst atrocities  that china has ever seen and  the death of millions of people and  and the disgrace and murder of some of  our top leaders  when that was all over  mao dead dung in power  every chinese i spoke to of whatever  generation whether it was of dung's  generation or the new  youngster just out of school said we've  learned our lesson there will never be  anything  the lies the government is telling are  the same techniques which were  used in the cultural revolution how can  they how can people make these mistakes  i think  they're made because  they misread  some essential ingredient ingredients in  human character they misread  in particular what revolves around power  and the extent to which a man who has  power  would use whatever means at his disposal  to hold that power even at great cost  i see dung as having used the power of  the military in the force of the  military and the shooting down of his  own citizens as a visual and vivid  demonstration that he is still in charge  and he's able to run that country with  the help of the army in spite of  anything people say  he's not alone in that use of power to  hold power i think that one has to  understand that anyone  who has absolute power as it tends to be  true in a dictatorship like china or the  soviet union will in the end use that  power use the power of illegal to keep  his personal power he identifies with  his country  and we must remember that  here's what the book looks like it's  both in paperback and in hardback  little brown publishes it ten of men  diary 13 days in june by harrison e  salisbury our guest for the last hour  thank you very much thank you  harrison salisbury former new york times  editor and author of tiananmen diary 13  days in june it's published by little  brown and company for more information  visit their website at twbookmark.com  every weekend book tv brings you an  encore presentation of book notes on  saturdays at 7 pm and sundays at 11 a.m  east
 

